2026-05-24 01:16:38,332 - INFO - Selected state: ("### Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nBelow are three carefully chosen next steps for feature engineering, taking advantage of the given operators to increase downstream model performance. The reasoning behind each step is tied to understanding molecular representation and enhancing feature interactions.\n\n---\n\n#### Step 1: Ratio of Oxygen Atoms to Carbon Atoms\npython\n# Feature name\ndf['O_C_ratio'] = df['nO'] / (df['nHM'] + 1e-6)\n# Feature description\n# This feature computes the ratio of oxygen atoms (nO) to heavy atoms (nHM) to capture the relative oxygen enrichment of the molecules.\n# Usefulness: Oxygen enrichment often impacts biodegradability, as oxygen is central to organic decomposition processes. This extracted ratio provides direct insight into this relationship.\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2: Log Transformation of Frequencies\npython\n# Feature name\ndf['log_F03[C-O]'] = df['F03[C-O]'].apply(lambda x: np.log(x + 1))\ndf['log_F04[C-N]'] = df['F04[C-N]'].apply(lambda x: np.log(x + 1))\n# Feature description\n# Log transformations of the frequencies of C-O and C-N bonds capture their distribution in a compressed, non-linear space.\n# Usefulness: Frequencies of specific bonds at different topological distances (C-O, C-N) are highly skewed; the log transformation stabilizes the variance and emphasizes relative importance in molecular composition.\nend\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3: Interaction between Biodegradability Relevant Factors\npython\n# Feature name\ndf['Biodegradability_strength'] = (df['nHDon'] * df['SdO']) / (df['nX'] + 1)\n# Feature description\n# This feature computes a composite metric by combining hydrogen bond donation (nHDon), sum of dO E-states (SdO), and halogen count (nX).\n# Usefulness: Hydrogen bonds and molecular oxygen states are indicators for chemical reactivity and decomposition, while halogens often inhibit biodegradability. This interaction enriches understanding of molecule class separation.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns (if irrelevant for model or noisy)\n#### Example Column Removal\npython\n# Explanation why the column Psi_i_1d is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Psi_i_1d'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: Psi_i_1d (Intrinsic state pseudoconnectivity index - type 1d) has a range with negligible variance around zero [-1.099, 1.073]. This suggests low predictive importance and potential noisiness in training.\nend\n\n#### Another Column Removal\npython\n# Explanation why the column nArCOOR is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['nArCOOR'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: nArCOOR (Number of esters - aromatic) has an extremely low mean (0.0506) and variance (std: 0.3039). Its presence rarely contributes to distinguishing chemical classes, adding limited value.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Final Thoughts\nThese engineered features tailor towards forming interactions and transforming high-value molecular descriptors directly related to biodegradability processes while eliminating irrelevant variables. This holistic approach aims to maximize predictive utility and improve downstream modeling performance.", "python\n# Step 1: Log Transformation on O_C_ratio\n# Feature name: log_O_C_ratio\n# Log transformation of O_C_ratio. This reduces the impact of extreme values (outliers) in a feature with a large range (e.g., 0 to 12,000,000), making it suitable for models sensitive to scaling.\n# Usefulness: A large range like O_C_ratio can create noise for models, while a log transformation maps the feature to a smaller scale, preserving relationships yet improving model stability.\n\ndf['log_O_C_ratio'] = df['O_C_ratio'].apply(lambda x: np.log1p(x))\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Interaction between nO and nHDon\n# Feature name: interaction_nO_nHDon\n# Multiplicative interaction between nO (number of oxygen atoms) and nHDon (number of donor atoms for H-bonds, N and O).\n# Usefulness: Oxygen atoms (nO) and hydrogen donors (nHDon) are chemically relevant for biodegradability. Their interaction might reveal synergies in molecular properties affecting classification.\n\ndf['interaction_nO_nHDon'] = df['nO'] * df['nHDon']\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Z-score Normalization for SdO\n# Feature name: zscore_SdO\n# Z-score normalization for SdO (Sum of dO E-states).\n# Usefulness: SdO has a wide range and is likely skewed, as the majority of its data lies near zero (Q1 = 0). Applying z-score normalizes the feature to have a mean of 0 and std=1, which can improve convergence and model performance by adjusting for outliers.\n\ndf['zscore_SdO'] = (df['SdO'] - df['SdO'].mean()) / df['SdO'].std()\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Biodegradability_strength is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Biodegradability_strength'], inplace=True)\n# Biodegradability_strength contains mainly zeros (Q1 = Q2 = Q3 = 0) with a high standard deviation (Std = 26.8425), meaning it provides little useful information (likely noise).\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column log_F04[C-N] is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['log_F04[C-N]'], inplace=True)\n# log_F04[C-N] mainly contains zeros or small values (Q1 = Q2 = 0.0), which may not carry significant predictive power for the downstream task.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column B04[C-Br] is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['B04[C-Br]'], inplace=True)\n# B04[C-Br] is a binary feature with a very low mean (mean = 0.0253), indicating that it is mostly zeros and not contributing useful variance to the model.\nend"), with improvements -
