ty.\n\ndf['ratio_TI2L_SpMaxL'] = df['TI2_L'] / df['SpMax_L']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column `log_O_C_ratio` is dropped\n# The feature `log_O_C_ratio` appears highly correlated with `O_C_ratio` (as it is a logarithmic transformation of the original variable). When used alongside its base feature, it may provide redundant information to the model, increasing complexity without improving performance. Dropping it can prevent overfitting and reduce feature redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['log_O_C_ratio'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column `zscore_SdO` is dropped\n# The feature `zscore_SdO` is a z-score normalization of `SdO`. If both the original feature `SdO` and its z-score version are present, this may confuse the model and introduce redundancy. Since `SdO` itself is already standardized and useful in its raw form, we can drop the z-score version.\ndf.drop(columns=['zscore_SdO'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column `B03[C-Cl]` is dropped\n# The feature `B03[C-Cl]` represents binary presence/absence of C-Cl at topological distance 3. The mean value of the feature is very close to 0 (mean=0.1596) with low variance (std=0.3665), indicating that this feature has minimal variance and thus limited predictive power in discriminating between classes. Removing it reduces noise.\ndf.drop(columns=['B03[C-Cl]'], inplace=True)\n```end", "```python\n# Step 1: Log Transformation of `O_C_ratio`\n# Feature name: log_O_C_ratio_transformed\n# Feature description: Apply a logarithmic transformation to the `O_C_ratio` feature, which has a large range and a long right tail. Logarithmic transformations can help compress the range of numeric features and make them more normally distributed, often improving model stability and performance.\n# Usefulness: The `O_C_ratio` attribute has a heavy right skew (large mean compared to Q3 and median). This transformation can normalize the distribution and make patterns in data more learnable by the model.\ndf['log_O_C_ratio_transformed'] = np.log1p(df['O_C_ratio'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: Interaction Term Between `nO` and `nHDon`\n# Feature name: interaction_nO_nHDon_squared\n# Feature description: Generating a new interaction feature combining the `nO` (number of oxygen atoms) and `nHDon` (number of donor atoms for H-bonds) by squaring their already-present interaction term (`interaction_nO_nHDon`).\n# Usefulness: Oxygen (nO) and donor H-bond atoms (nHDon) are chemically significant in determining molecular behavior, including their biodegradability. By squaring their interaction, we increase the weight of instances where both attributes are present in higher magnitudes, which could be key for classification.\ndf['interaction_nO_nHDon_squared'] = df['interaction_nO_nHDon'] ** 2\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Ratio of `SdO` to `SpMax_L`\n# Feature name: ratio_SdO_to_SpMax_L\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of `SdO` (Sum of dO E-states) to `SpMax_L` (Leading eigenvalue from Laplace matrix). This ratio could capture a normalized E-state relevance weighted by structural connectivity density.\n# Usefulness: `SdO` is related to electronic distribution (E-states), while `SpMax_L` relates to molecular topology. Their combination may help identify how the electronic distribution relates to the molecule's structure, contributing more context to biodegradability predictions.\ndf['ratio_SdO_to_SpMax_L'] = df['SdO'] / (df['SpMax_L'] + 1e-8)  # Adding epsilon to prevent divide-by-zero errors\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping Columns: Potential collinearity or redundancy in existing features\n# Explanation why the column `log_O_C_ratio` is dropped\n# Rationale: Since we created a more effective and likely normalized version of `log_O_C_ratio` in step 1 (`log_O_C_ratio_transformed`), the original version may now be redundant and could introduce collinearity issues.\ndf.drop(columns=['log_O_C_ratio'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping Columns: Redundant and low-variance feature\n# Explanation why the column `interaction_nO_nHDon` is dropped\n# Rationale: Squaring the `interaction_nO_nHDon` term (step 2) provides an enhanced form of this feature. The original can now be considered redundant.\ndf.drop(columns=['interaction_nO_nHDon'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
